message.content:
Below is a concise way to decide whether a *synthetic* rest‑parameter needs to be injected into a TypeScript/JavaScript function declaration when the **last** declared parameter is already variadic (i.e., uses a spread operator or a JSDoc tag such as `@variadic`).  

---

### 1️⃣ What “synthetic” means here  
When compiling TS → JS, the compiler sometimes adds an extra rest‑parameter (`…rest`) behind the scenes so that runtime calls receive all trailing arguments correctly.  
If your source already declares a variadic argument (`function foo(...x)` or `/** @param {...any[]} x */`), there’s usually **no need** to inject this synthetic one again—doing so would duplicate the semantics and potentially break overload resolution.

---

### 2️⃣ Decision algorithm (in plain English)

| Step | Condition | Action |
|------|-----------|--------|
| 1 | Is the **last** formal parameter a native rest parameter (`...p`)? | ✅ Skip injection. |
| 2 | Does the last parameter carry a JSDoc tag indicating variability (`@var`, `@variadic`, `@arrayof`, …)? | ✅ Skip injection. |
| 3 | Otherwise | ❌ Inject a synthetic rest parameter at the end of the parameter list. |

> **Why step 2 matters:** Even if the source doesn’t explicitly write `...p`, developers may annotate a normal parameter as variadic via JSDoc. In that case the compiler treats it like a rest parameter internally.

---

### 3️⃣ Using the helper function

Assuming you’re working inside a Node.js environment where the `functions.maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter` API is available:

```js
// Example inputs:
const funcDeclaration = {
    name: "myFunc",
    kind: "FunctionExpression",   // whatever shape your AST node takes
};

let funcParameters = [
    /* existing params */
];

// Call the helper
functions.maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter({
    declaration: JSON.stringify(funcDeclaration),
    parameters: JSON.stringify(funcParameters)
});

// After execution:
// - If a synthetic rest was required, `funcParameters` will now contain an additional element.
// - If not, `funcParameters` remains unchanged.
```

> **Note:** The helper mutates the passed-in `parameters` array directly. Make sure you pass a mutable reference rather than a copy if you intend to observe changes.

---

### 4️⃣ Quick sanity test

Here’s a tiny self‑contained demo showing both scenarios:

```js
const fs = require("fs");
const path = require("path");

// Mock implementation of the helper (replace with real import):
const maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter = ({ declaration, parameters }) => {
    const declObj = JSON.parse(declaration);
    let paramsArr = JSON.parse(parameters);

    // Simulate detection of variadic types
    const lastParam = paramsArr[paramsArr.length - 1];
    const isVariadic =
        typeof lastParam === "string" && lastParam.startsWith("...");

    if (!isVariadic) {
        // Add synthetic rest
        paramsArr.push("{synthetic_rest}");
    }

    return paramsArr;
};

// Test cases
console.log(
    maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter({
        declaration: '{"name":"f","kind":"Fn"}',
        parameters: '["a", "...b"]'
    })
); // ➜ ["a", "...b"]

console.log(
    maybeAddJsSyntheticRestParameter({
        declaration: '{"name":"g","kind":"Fn"}',
        parameters: '["c", "d"]'
    })
); // ➜ ["c", "d", "{synthetic_rest}"]
```

In the first log, the last parameter is already variadic (`"...b"`), so nothing gets appended.  
In the second log, since neither the last parameter nor any JSDoc indicates variance, the helper appends `{synthetic_rest}`.

---

#### TL;DR

- **Do NOT** add a synthetic rest if the last parameter is already variadic (via `...` or appropriate JSDoc).  
- Use the helper above to perform the check automatically—it mutates the original parameters array only when truly needed.
